imageconvert.pl -- encode an image as a base64 blob for embedding in Perl
=========================================================================

fetchconfig-web inlines two small images -- the title-bar logo and the login
icon -- directly in fetchconfig-web.cgi as base64 constants. The reason is load
performance, not packaging: an inlined image travels inside the HTML of the
first response, so it costs no second HTTP request and is painted with the page
rather than one round trip later. The title-bar logo earns its place twice over,
as it is also the cid: inline image of the report email, where a link to a web-
server file is not a usable option.

imageconvert.pl generates those constants: it reads an image and writes a ready-
to-paste Perl snippet that assigns the image, base64-encoded, to a scalar
variable.

The application is not a single self-contained file. The stylesheet, the fonts,
favicon.ico, the help tree and the login backdrop back.jpg (roughly 600 KB) are
all deployed as ordinary files under WEB_DIR and served from the web server --
see CSS_BASE_URL, FONT_BASE_URL, IMAGE_BASE_URL and HELP_BASE_URL in the main
README. Inlining is reserved for images that are small and needed in the first
paint of the page that uses them. Anything larger belongs on disk: base64
inflates the payload by about a third, an inlined image cannot be cached by the
browser, and that cost is paid again on every single page load.

Requirements
============

Perl 5 with the core module MIME::Base64 (part of the standard library since
Perl 5.8, so no extra installation is normally needed).

Usage
=====

    imageconvert.pl <input-image> <output-file> <var-name>

  Argument: input-image
  Meaning: Path to the image (or any binary file) to encode.

  Argument: output-file
  Meaning: File to write the generated Perl snippet to. Use - to write to
           standard output instead.

  Argument: var-name
  Meaning: The Perl scalar variable name for the blob, e.g. LOGO_BASE64. A
           leading $ is accepted and ignored. It must be a valid Perl identifier
           (letters, digits, underscore; not starting with a digit).

Options:

Option         Effect
-------------  --------------------------------
-h, --help     Show the built-in help and exit.
-V, --version  Show the version and exit.

Output format
=============

The generated snippet looks exactly like this (base64 wrapped at 80 columns):

    my $LOGO_BASE64 = do {
        local $/;
        my $raw = <<'B64';
    iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAADIAAAAyCAYAAAAeP4ixAAAWfmNhQlgAABZ+anVtYgAAAB5qdW1kYzJw
    ... (many more 80-character lines) ...
    B64
        $raw =~ s/\s+//g;
        $raw;
    };

The heredoc keeps the base64 readable in the source (wrapped lines); the
s/\s+//g at the end strips the newlines at run time so $LOGO_BASE64 holds one
continuous base64 string. Use it wherever the app needs the image, e.g.:

    <img src="data:image/png;base64,$LOGO_BASE64" alt="logo">

Examples
========

Write the snippet to a file:

    imageconvert.pl logo.png logo-b64.pl LOGO_BASE64

Write to standard output (e.g. to pipe or redirect):

    imageconvert.pl back.jpg - IMAGE_BASE64 > back-b64.pl

Then open the output, copy the whole my $VAR = do { ... }; block, and paste it
into fetchconfig-web.cgi in place of the existing constant.

Exit codes
==========

Code  Meaning
----  --------------------------------------------------------------
0     success (or --help / --version)
1     no arguments given (usage shown)
2     an error occurred (a descriptive message is printed to stderr)

Error conditions are reported clearly, for example: input file missing, not
readable, empty, or a directory; invalid variable name; output file not
writable. On any error, nothing partial is left in the output file only if the
open itself failed; if a write fails midway the message says so.

